1e:["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"The Quran was revealed gradually over 23 years. The Makki period (610–622 CE, 13 years) addressed the polytheists of Makkah with short, powerful, and stirring verses on tawheed, the hereafter, and the stories of the prophets — often beginning with \"Ya ayyuhan-nas\" (O mankind)."}]
1f:["$","p","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"The Madani period (622–632 CE, 10 years) addressed the Muslims and the People of the Book with longer, detailed, legislative verses on law, society, and treaties — often beginning with \"Ya ayyuhal-ladhina amanu\" (O you who believe)."}]
20:["$","p","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"During the Prophet’s ﷺ lifetime the Quran was written down but not yet gathered in one place. More than 40 scribes (Kuttab-e-Wahi) recorded the revelation as it descended."}]
21:["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-8 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2","children":[["$","li","Flat stones (Laukh / Hajarah)",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Flat stones (Laukh / Hajarah)"]}],["$","li","Fresh palm branches (Usub al-Nakhl)",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Fresh palm branches (Usub al-Nakhl)"]}],["$","li","Bones of camels and horses (Azam)",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Bones of camels and horses (Azam)"]}],["$","li","Camel hide (Adeem)",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Camel hide (Adeem)"]}],["$","li","Pieces of cloth (Riqaa)",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Pieces of cloth (Riqaa)"]}],["$","li","Silver tablets (Alwah min Fidda)",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Silver tablets (Alwah min Fidda)"]}]]}]
22:["$","section","aakhri-wahi",{"id":"aakhri-wahi","aria-labelledby":"aakhri-wahi-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Aakhri Wahi"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"aakhri-wahi-title","className":"mt-1 text-balance font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-foreground","children":"The Final Revelation"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-pretty text-muted-foreground","children":"The completion of the divine message"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"According to the well-known position of the scholars, the final verse of the Quran was revealed at Arafah during the Farewell Pilgrimage (Hujjat ul-Wada’) on 9 Dhul Hijjah, 10 AH (18 March 632 CE)."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"The Prophet ﷺ asked the gathering: \"Have I conveyed the message? O Allah, bear witness.\" He passed away only 81 days later, on 12 Rabi ul-Awwal 11 AH. Upon hearing this verse, Umar (RA) wept — sensing that the completion of the religion signalled the approaching departure of the Messenger ﷺ."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"During the Prophet’s ﷺ era the Quran was preserved by two mutually confirming methods: memorization (hifz) by over a thousand Companions, and writing (kitabah) by 40+ scribes. Every Ramadan the Prophet ﷺ recited the entire Quran to Jibra’eel — and twice in his final year (Dour-e-Akheer)."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"mt-8 rounded-lg border border-primary/30 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center","children":[["$","p",null,{"lang":"ar","dir":"rtl","className":"text-2xl leading-loose text-foreground","children":"الْيَوْمَ أَكْمَلْتُ لَكُمْ دِينَكُمْ وَأَتْمَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ نِعْمَتِي"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-sm italic text-muted-foreground","children":"Al-yawma akmaltu lakum deenakum wa atmamtu 'alaykum ni'mati"}],["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"mt-2 font-medium text-foreground","children":["\"","This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favour upon you","\""]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-xs text-primary","children":"Surah Al-Ma'idah (5:3) — Day of Arafah, 9 Dhul Hijjah, 10 AH (632 CE)"}]]}],"$undefined","$undefined"]}]
23:["$","section","pehla-jamaa",{"id":"pehla-jamaa","aria-labelledby":"pehla-jamaa-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Pehla Jamaa' — Abu Bakr (RA) ka Daur"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"pehla-jamaa-title","className":"mt-1 text-balance font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-foreground","children":"The First Compilation"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-pretty text-muted-foreground","children":"632–634 CE | The first caliph orders the Quran gathered into one collection"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"After the Prophet’s ﷺ passing, the false prophet Musaylimah rebelled. At the Battle of Yamama (633 CE) more than 1,200 Muslims were martyred — among them over 70 huffaz. Umar (RA) urged Abu Bakr (RA) to gather the Quran in one place before more of its carriers were lost."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Abu Bakr (RA) initially hesitated: \"How can I do something the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not do?\" But Allah opened his heart to it, and he entrusted the task to Zaid ibn Thabit (RA) — the foremost scribe of revelation — who said the task felt heavier than being asked to move a mountain."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Zaid (RA) applied the strictest methodology: he accepted only what was written down with two witnesses AND confirmed by the memory of the huffaz, and only what had been dictated as revelation in the Prophet’s ﷺ presence. The final two verses of Surah At-Tawbah were found in writing only with Khuzaymah al-Ansari (RA), whose testimony the Prophet ﷺ had declared equal to two men."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"mt-8 rounded-lg border border-primary/30 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center","children":[["$","p",null,{"lang":"ar","dir":"rtl","className":"text-2xl leading-loose text-foreground","children":"كَيْفَ أَفْعَلُ شَيْئًا لَمْ يَفْعَلْهُ رَسُولُ اللَّهِ ﷺ"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-sm italic text-muted-foreground","children":"Kayfa af'alu shay'an lam yaf'alhu Rasoolullahi ﷺ"}],["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"mt-2 font-medium text-foreground","children":["\"","How can I do something the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not do?","\""]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-xs text-primary","children":"The first response of Abu Bakr (RA) — Sahih Bukhari"}]]}],["$","dl",null,{"className":"mt-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2","children":[["$","div","Result",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Result"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":"The Quran gathered for the first time into Suhuf (sheets)"}]]}],["$","div","Custody",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Custody"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":"Kept by Abu Bakr (RA), then Umar (RA), then Umm ul-Mu’mineen Hafsa (RA)"}]]}],["$","div","Legacy",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Legacy"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":"These Suhuf later became the foundation of the Uthmani Mushaf"}]]}]]}],"$undefined"]}]
24:["$","section","doosra-jamaa",{"id":"doosra-jamaa","aria-labelledby":"doosra-jamaa-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Doosra Jamaa' — Uthman (RA) ka Daur"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"doosra-jamaa-title","className":"mt-1 text-balance font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-foreground","children":"The Standardization"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-pretty text-muted-foreground","children":"644–656 CE | 'Jaami' ul-Quran' — one standard Mushaf for the whole Ummah"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman (RA), campaigning with the armies of Syria and Iraq, saw Muslims disputing over differing recitations — Iraq following Ibn Mas’ood (RA), Syria following Ubayy ibn Ka’b (RA), Madinah following Zaid ibn Thabit (RA). He rushed to Uthman (RA): \"O Commander of the Faithful, save this Ummah before they differ over the Book as the Jews and Christians did!\""}],["$","p","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Uthman (RA) consulted the Companions and, by consensus, formed a committee headed by Zaid ibn Thabit (RA) with Abdullah ibn Zubayr, Sa’eed ibn al-’As, and Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Harith (RA). The Suhuf of Hafsa (RA) served as the foundation; where dialects differed, the Qurayshi form was preferred; only mutawatir (mass-transmitted) readings were accepted; and dots and vowel marks were omitted so the valid readings could all be accommodated."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Five to nine copies were prepared and dispatched with a teacher-reciter to Makkah, Madinah (the Mushaf-e-Imam), Kufa, Basra, and Damascus — and by some reports Bahrain and Yemen. Divergent private copies were destroyed to prevent confusion. The Mushaf-e-Uthmani remains the standard to this day."}]]}],"$undefined","$undefined","$undefined"]}]
25:["$","section","aham-sahaba",{"id":"aham-sahaba","aria-labelledby":"aham-sahaba-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Jamaa'-e-Quran ke Aham Sahaba"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"aham-sahaba-title","className":"mt-1 text-balance font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-foreground","children":"The Key Companions"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-pretty text-muted-foreground","children":"The blessed personalities behind the preservation"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Zaid ibn Thabit (RA) is the central figure: chosen as a scribe of revelation at just 17, present at the Prophet’s ﷺ final review of the Quran, leader of both compilations, and instructed by the Prophet ﷺ to learn the Torah’s script and Syriac."}]]}],"$undefined","$undefined",["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-8 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2","children":[["$","li","Zaid ibn Thabit (RA) — scribe of revelation, led both the first and second compilations",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Zaid ibn Thabit (RA) — scribe of revelation, led both the first and second compilations"]}],["$","li","Abu Bakr Siddiq (RA) — gave the first command to gather the Quran",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Abu Bakr Siddiq (RA) — gave the first command to gather the Quran"]}],["$","li","Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) — first proposed the compilation and safeguarded the Suhuf",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) — first proposed the compilation and safeguarded the Suhuf"]}],["$","li","Uthman ibn Affan (RA) — \"Jaami’ ul-Quran\", standardized the Mushaf for the whole world",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Uthman ibn Affan (RA) — \"Jaami’ ul-Quran\", standardized the Mushaf for the whole world"]}],["$","li","Hafsa bint Umar (RA) — trusted custodian of the Suhuf between the two compilations",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Hafsa bint Umar (RA) — trusted custodian of the Suhuf between the two compilations"]}],["$","li","Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA) — scribe of revelation who upheld the Uthmani Mushaf in his caliphate",{"className":"flex items-start gap-2 rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-0.5 text-primary","children":"✦"}],"Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA) — scribe of revelation who upheld the Uthmani Mushaf in his caliphate"]}]]}]]}]
26:["$","section","qiraat",{"id":"qiraat","aria-labelledby":"qiraat-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Ilm-e-Qira'at"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"qiraat-title","className":"mt-1 text-balance font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-foreground","children":"The Science of Qira’at"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-pretty text-muted-foreground","children":"Seven ahruf, ten canonical readings — all traced to the Prophet ﷺ"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"The Quran was revealed upon seven ahruf (modes), from which the famous canonical Qira’at emerged — each with an unbroken chain of transmission to the Prophet ﷺ: Nafi’ (Madinah), Ibn Katheer (Makkah), Abu Amr (Basra), Ibn Aamir (Sham), Asim (Kufa), Hamza (Kufa), and Kisai (Kufa)."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Today the riwayah of Hafs ’an Asim is the most widespread in the world, while Warsh ’an Nafi’ is recited across North and West Africa."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"mt-8 rounded-lg border border-primary/30 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center","children":[["$","p",null,{"lang":"ar","dir":"rtl","className":"text-2xl leading-loose text-foreground","children":"إِنَّ هَذَا الْقُرْآنَ أُنْزِلَ عَلَى سَبْعَةِ أَحْرُفٍ"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-sm italic text-muted-foreground","children":"Inna haadhal Qur'ana unzila 'alaa sab'ati ahruf"}],["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"mt-2 font-medium text-foreground","children":["\"","Indeed, this Quran was revealed upon seven ahruf","\""]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-xs text-primary","children":"Sahih Bukhari & Sahih Muslim — Mutawatir hadith"}]]}],"$undefined","$undefined"]}]
27:["$","section","hifazat",{"id":"hifazat","aria-labelledby":"hifazat-title","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-primary","children":"Hifazat-e-Quran — Allah ka Wada"}],["$","h2",null,{"id":"hifazat-title","className":"mt-1 text-balance font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-foreground","children":"The Divine Promise of Preservation"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-pretty text-muted-foreground","children":"A promise being fulfilled for over 1,400 years"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"The Quran is the only book in history preserved letter-for-letter for over fourteen centuries. More than ten million huffaz across the world carry it in their hearts; children still memorize it completely by the age of seven to nine; and every Ramadan it is recited in full in Taraweeh across the globe. Every hafiz’s chain of transmission (isnad) reaches back to the Prophet ﷺ himself."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"mt-8 rounded-lg border border-primary/30 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center","children":[["$","p",null,{"lang":"ar","dir":"rtl","className":"text-2xl leading-loose text-foreground","children":"إِنَّا نَحْنُ نَزَّلْنَا الذِّكْرَ وَإِنَّا لَهُ لَحَافِظُونَ"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-sm italic text-muted-foreground","children":"Innaa nahnu nazzalnad-dhikra wa innaa lahu la-haafizoon"}],["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"mt-2 font-medium text-foreground","children":["\"","Indeed, it is We who sent down the Reminder, and indeed, We will be its Guardian","\""]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-xs text-primary","children":"Surah Al-Hijr (15:9) — Allah's promise, fulfilled to this day"}]]}],"$undefined","$undefined"]}]
